criteria.descriptionMarkdown:
A local release-plz preview with current release-plz (record `release-plz --version`; use the latest release, which is what `release-plz/action@v0.5` installs). In a throwaway clone under the ignored `artifacts-temp/agentx/signalr-releases/` carrying this epic's `release-plz.toml`, version `1.0.0`, the CHANGELOG header and a `## [1.0.0]` section, committed and tagged `v1.0.0` locally, `release-plz update --config release-plz.toml` after each case (reset to the tag between cases) shows:

1. release-plz reads the configuration without error (every key accepted, including `pr_branch_prefix`; if that key is rejected, drop it, keep the default prefix and record it);
2. `docs: x`, `docs(agentx): x` and `test(e2e): x` → no version change and no CHANGELOG change;
3. `docs: y` then `fix(client): x` → `1.0.1`; CHANGELOG.md gets `## [1.0.1] - <today>` between the header and `## [1.0.0]`, the header appears once, the `docs` line is listed and a `docs(agentx)` line is not;
4. `feat(client): x` → `1.1.0`, and a `feat(client): x` whose body describes a breaking API change and ends with a `Migration: <note>` footer → `1.1.0` (the 1.x policy: a break typed `feat` bumps the minor), with the CHANGELOG line of that commit followed by the indented `- Migration: <note>` line;
5. with `semver_check = false`, a `fix(client): x` commit that removes or narrows a public item → `1.0.1` (no cargo-semver-checks run forces a major);
6. **Cargo.lock**: with `Cargo.lock` git-ignored and absent, release-plz succeeds and `git status --porcelain` lists only `Cargo.toml` and `CHANGELOG.md`, so `Cargo.lock` stays ignored and a release PR changes only those two files. If release-plz fails without a tracked lockfile, record the error and ask the owner before tracking it.

The commands, tool version and observed versions/diffs go in the task handoff; raw output stays in `artifacts-temp/`. Nothing from the clone is pushed. Verified by the recorded results.

`.github/workflows/release-plz.yml`, modelled on RayX.CLI's and adapted to this repository's governance:

- `on`: `push` with `branches: [master]`, and `workflow_dispatch`; no `pull_request`, `pull_request_target` or `schedule`;
- top-level `permissions: contents: read`; job `release` (`name: release-plz / release`) with `contents: write`; job `release-pr` (`name: release-plz / release-pr`) with `contents: write`, `pull-requests: write` and `needs: release` (as in Gpux: on every push, above all the first one whose untagged history still holds pre-1.0 `feat(client)!` commits, the release PR is computed only after `release` has tagged); each job `if: github.repository == 'rayx-ui/RayX.SignalR'`, `runs-on: ubuntu-latest`, and its own concurrency group with `cancel-in-progress: false`;
- steps per job: `actions/checkout@v7` with `fetch-depth: 0` and `token: ${{ secrets.RELEASE_PLZ_TOKEN }}`; `run: rustup toolchain install 1.92.0 --profile minimal` (the pinned toolchain, as the other workflows install it; no third-party Rust action); `release-plz/action@v0.5` with `command: release` or `command: release-pr` and `env: GITHUB_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.RELEASE_PLZ_TOKEN }}`;
- `RELEASE_PLZ_TOKEN` is the only secret; no `dotnet`, Aspire, Node, pnpm, Playwright or `cargo test` (it runs no tests and needs only Rust; Quality's light jobs test `master` pushes); no upload steps; the word `publish` appears nowhere, comments included; comments say why the PAT is needed (events made with the default token start no workflows).

Verified by the new group `Case=release-automation` and the YAML duplicate-key check.

Consumers depend on a release tag only:

- the README `## Installation` section says the crate is consumed from Git by release tag (not from crates.io or any registry), shows `rayx-signalr = { git = "https://github.com/rayx-ui/RayX.SignalR", tag = "v1.0.0", features = ["messagepack"] }`, says to pick the latest tag from the Releases page, and links `docs/releasing.md`;
- every `rayx-signalr = { … }` dependency snippet in `README.md`, `docs/*.md` (today `01-getting-started.md` and `08-tls.md`) and `.claude/skills/rayx-signalr/**/*.md` (today `SKILL.md`, `references/setup-and-connection.md`, `references/tls.md`, including the abbreviated `git = "..."` forms) carries `tag = "vX.Y.Z"`; no consumer snippet uses `path = "../RayX.SignalR"` or an untagged `git` (the repository-internal `tests/wasm-fixture` path dependency is not consumer documentation);
- the skill's `SKILL.md` no longer says the crate needs a Git or path dependency or that RayX consumes it by path.

Verified by the new group `Case=release-consumption` and the existing `RepositoryTests.DocumentationDescribesTheAspireTestWorkflow` (README and docs index stay valid).

`docs/releasing.md` explains, for the owner and agents:

- how a release happens: a push to `master` runs the light Quality jobs and `release-plz / release-pr`, which opens or updates the release PR (version bump in `Cargo.toml`, new `CHANGELOG.md` section); before merging, check that the proposed version is still `1.x`, that the PR changes only `Cargo.toml` and `CHANGELOG.md`, and that `quality / rustfmt` and `quality / clippy` are green; merging is outward-facing (an agent merges only with the owner's go-ahead); the merge push runs `release-plz / release`, which tags `vX.Y.Z` and creates the GitHub Release with that section as notes; v1.0.0 needed no PR (git-only initial release);
- the **1.x policy**: while the owner is the only consumer no release leaves 1.x; no commit carries `!` or a `BREAKING CHANGE:` footer; a breaking change is a `feat` (or `fix`) whose body states the break, with consumer notes in a `Migration:` footer that the changelog renders under the commit; a `2.0.0` release-PR proposal means a stray `!`/`BREAKING CHANGE:` commit, which is reworded (force push allowed) instead of merging; `semver_check` is off for the same reason; the policy ends when a second consumer appears, and then `!` and major bumps return;
- the commit-type table: `fix`/`perf` → patch, `feat` → minor (also for a described break); `docs`, `test`, `ci`, `build`, `chore` and `refactor` start no release and ride along in the next one; `docs(agentx)`, `chore(agentx)`, `chore(release)` and `chore(deps)` never appear in the changelog; test-only or CI-only fixes use `test`/`ci`, not `fix`;
- why `Cargo.lock` stays git-ignored (RPZ-2);
- `RELEASE_PLZ_TOKEN`: one fine-grained PAT (resource owner `rayx-ui`; the four library repositories; Contents and Pull requests read/write) stored as a repository secret or an organization secret shared with this repository; why the default `GITHUB_TOKEN` is not enough; that release PRs and tags then appear as the token's owner; that on expiry the Release-plz runs go red and releases silently stop; how to rotate it;
- the by-hand fallback: bump `Cargo.toml`, add the `CHANGELOG.md` section, commit and push per the push policy, `git tag vX.Y.Z`, `git push origin vX.Y.Z`, `gh release create vX.Y.Z --notes-file <section>`; release-plz continues from that tag;
- the `v*` tag ruleset blocks moving and deleting tags: a bad release is fixed by a new patch release, never by retagging;
- the push policy (fetch, rebase onto `origin/master`, `git push --force-with-lease origin master` with the owner's credentials, which the `DefaultProtection` ruleset lets through; no feature branches or PRs for implementation; PRs only for releases), that a push to `master` runs only rustfmt, clippy and the Rust unit tests (the full Quality matrices run on pull requests), and that release PRs run only `quality / rustfmt` and `quality / clippy`;
- how consumers pin and bump the tag (link to the README).

`docs/README.md` links it in Part IV and the README Installation section links it. Verified by review.

`CLAUDE.md` and `AGENTS.md` stay byte-identical and:

- Repository Layout: `publish = false` because releases are git tags `vX.Y.Z` and never a registry (link `docs/releasing.md`), replacing "until a registry and release policy exist";
- a short `## Releases` section: Conventional Commits drive the version (`fix`/`perf` patch, `feat` minor); the 1.x policy (never `!` or `BREAKING CHANGE:`; a break is a `feat` or `fix` whose body states it, with consumer notes in a `Migration:` footer; ends when a second consumer appears); test- or CI-only fixes use `test`/`ci`; a release is merging release-plz's release PR (with the owner's go-ahead, after checking the version is `1.x` and only `Cargo.toml`/`CHANGELOG.md` changed); the push policy; link to `docs/releasing.md`;
- Consumers: consumers pin a release tag; RayX (`../RayX`) wraps the crate in `rayx_devkit::SignalRService` and moves from its untagged git dependency to a tag in its own epic; RayX.GameX (`../RayX.GameX`) depends on it by path with `messagepack` and checks this repository out in its CI (correcting the stale "RayX depends by path").

`docs/14-testing.md` "CI checks": Quality runs on pull requests and on pushes to `master` (a push runs only `quality / rustfmt`, `quality / clippy` and `quality / unit-contract`), End-to-end on pull requests and dispatch, Performance on dispatch; `release-plz.yml` also runs on pushes to `master` and runs no tests (`release-plz / release`, `release-plz / release-pr`); release PRs run only `quality / rustfmt` and `quality / clippy`; the "a merge to `master` triggers nothing" sentence goes; the `WorkflowContract` and `Repository` rows of the category table mention the release contracts; every string `RepositoryTests` requires stays. The skill's `references/maintaining.md` agrees wherever it describes CI triggers. Verified by review (the docs contract keeps the testing chapter's required strings).

The epic's commits reach `master` in one push (pre-approved by the owner): `git fetch origin`; `origin/master` is an ancestor of `HEAD` (otherwise stop and ask); `git rebase origin/master` (a no-op); `git push --force-with-lease origin master`. Before the push the `## [1.0.0]` date equals the push day. Then:

- the Release-plz run for that push is green, and its `release` job pushed tag `v1.0.0` pointing at the pushed commit whose `Cargo.toml` says `1.0.0`. If the run fails at checkout or push (the PAT was created for "only RayX.CLI" and may lack access to RayX.SignalR), the implementer reports the failing step to the owner (fix: add the repository to the PAT) and re-runs it with `gh run rerun` or `gh workflow run release-plz.yml --ref master` only after the owner says it is fixed, never blindly;
- the GitHub Release `v1.0.0` exists, is neither a draft nor a prerelease, is marked Latest, and its notes are the `## [1.0.0]` section (if release-plz left them empty, the implementer sets them from the section only with the owner's go-ahead and records the deviation);
- `release-pr` ran after `release` (`needs: release`) and no open release PR proposes a version (an unexpected one is reported and, with the owner's go-ahead, closed and recorded);
- the Quality run of the push has green `quality / rustfmt`, `quality / clippy` and both `quality / unit-contract` jobs and every other Quality job skipped; a red light job goes through the repair loop with its narrowest selector; no End-to-end or Performance run started for the push.

If release-plz cannot cut v1.0.0, the documented by-hand fallback cuts it with the owner's go-ahead and the deviation is recorded. Verified by `gh run view`, `gh release view v1.0.0 --json`, `git ls-remote --tags origin v1.0.0` and `gh pr list` output.
